==German==

===Etymology===
Via {{etyl|gmh|de}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|turi|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} *''duri-'' {{recons|durz|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{recons|dʰwer-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|dʰwor-||doorway, door, gate|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|dhur-|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Middle Dutch ''[[dore]]'' (Dutch ''[[deur]]''), Old Norse ''[[dyrr]]'' (Swedish ''[[dörr]]'', Danish {{term|dør|lang=da}}).  The Germanic word also existed in different stem-endings: ''*dora-'', {{recons|duran|lang=gem-pro}} (German ''[[Tor]]'', Old English ''[[dor]]'' > English {{term|door|lang=en}}), ''*duru-'' (Old English ''[[duru]]'').

